Chapter: DeskWindowFlickTest
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopDesk
  cites Works chapter GopFont
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A flick is a drag whose release carries speed AND whose pointer keeps going
 afterwards. Every arm of that is a comparison over integers, so the whole
 decision is decidable here with no screen, no pointer and no clock.

 IT CALLS THE FUNCTIONS `desk-wnd-flick-step` ITSELF CALLS. The step reads a
 mouse and a `ds` block, so its decision was named as `dk-flick-verdict` and
 its parts as `dk-flick-fast`, `dk-flick-edge`, `dk-flick-through` and
 `dk-flick-expired`. A test that re-derived those comparisons beside the step
 would be a SHADOW of them, free to drift while both stayed green.

 THE ROWS PRINT THE PAYLOAD. A row that printed "flicked" and nothing else
 would agree with an implementation that flicked on every release, which is
 the one wrong answer this gesture can give that still looks like a gesture.

 TIME IS PASSED IN AS TICKS AND A RATE, and every row below uses a rate of
 1000 so a tick reads as a millisecond and the arithmetic is checkable by eye
 rather than merely stable.

Section: Body

  fk-yn : Boolean -> Text
  fk-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

  fk-tps : Integer = 1000

  fk-edge-name : Integer -> Text
  fk-edge-name (e) =
    if e == dk-edge-left then "left"
    else if e == dk-edge-right then "right"
    else if e == dk-edge-top then "top"
    else if e == dk-edge-bottom then "bottom"
    else "?"

 THE DOMINANT AXIS NAMES THE EDGE. Each pair is chosen so the two candidate
 answers DISAGREE: no operand has equal components except the tie row, which
 exists to pin which way the tie is broken rather than to leave it to whoever
 reads the code next.

  fk-dir : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  fk-dir (label) (dx) (dy) (want) =
    label & ": " & integer-to-text dx & "," & integer-to-text dy
    & " -> " & fk-edge-name (dk-flick-edge dx dy)
    & "  code " & integer-to-text (dk-flick-edge dx dy)
    & "  wanted " & fk-edge-name want
    & "  " & fk-yn (dk-flick-edge dx dy == want)

 SPEED AT THE RELEASE. 700 device pixels a second against a rate of 1000, so
 60 pixels in 50 ms is 1200 and passes, 20 in 50 is 400 and does not, and 35
 in 50 is exactly 700 and passes because the comparison is `>=`. The three
 sit either side of the threshold rather than all above it, which is what
 makes the row an assertion instead of a demonstration.

  fk-fast : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> Text
  fk-fast (label) (dx) (dy) (el) (tps) (want) =
    label & ": " & integer-to-text dx & "," & integer-to-text dy
    & " over " & integer-to-text el & " at rate " & integer-to-text tps
    & " -> " & fk-yn (dk-flick-fast dx dy el tps)
    & "  wanted " & fk-yn want
    & "  " & fk-yn (dk-flick-fast dx dy el tps == want)

 THE FOLLOW-THROUGH, AND ITS CONTROL IS THE ROW THAT MATTERS. `stopped` is
 Damian's drag for moving purposes: released and then still. `reversed` is
 the other way a null can arrive, and without it a rule reading the magnitude
 and not the sign would pass every row above.

  fk-through : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> Text
  fk-through (label) (dir) (dx) (dy) (reach) (want) =
    label & ": " & fk-edge-name dir & " by " & integer-to-text dx & "," & integer-to-text dy
    & " reach " & integer-to-text reach
    & " -> " & fk-yn (dk-flick-through dir dx dy reach)
    & "  wanted " & fk-yn want
    & "  " & fk-yn (dk-flick-through dir dx dy reach == want)

 THE VERDICT IS THE FUNCTION THE STEP BRANCHES ON, and it has three answers.
 `wait` and `drop` are the pair a boolean could not tell apart: the hand is
 still moving, against the window closed and this was a move.

  fk-vname : Integer -> Text
  fk-vname (v) =
    if v == dk-flick-dock then "dock"
    else if v == dk-flick-drop then "drop"
    else "wait"

  fk-verdict : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  fk-verdict (label) (dir) (dx) (dy) (el) (tps) (want) =
    let got = dk-flick-verdict dir dx dy 48 el tps
    in label & ": " & fk-edge-name dir & " by " & integer-to-text dx & "," & integer-to-text dy
       & " at " & integer-to-text el & " ms -> " & fk-vname got
       & "  wanted " & fk-vname want
       & "  " & fk-yn (got == want)

 THE ARM ROUND-TRIPS THROUGH THE CELLS. The release point is packed into one
 cell, so the pair is chosen to disagree the way the drag offset's is: 1300
 and 7 cannot both come back right from a packer that answered one field for
 both, and 1300 is wider than a byte.

  fk-arm : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  fk-arm (ds) (fid) (x) (y) =
    let a = dk-flick-arm ds fid dk-edge-top x y 500
    in "armed " & integer-to-text (dk-flick-fid ds)
       & " dir " & fk-edge-name (peek-32 ds dk-flick-dir-cell)
       & " at " & integer-to-text (dk-pt-x (peek-32 ds dk-flick-org-cell))
       & "," & integer-to-text (dk-pt-y (peek-32 ds dk-flick-org-cell))
       & "  exact " & fk-yn (dk-flick-fid ds == fid
                             & peek-32 ds dk-flick-dir-cell == dk-edge-top
                             & dk-pt-x (peek-32 ds dk-flick-org-cell) == x
                             & dk-pt-y (peek-32 ds dk-flick-org-cell) == y)

  fk-disarm : Integer -> Text
  fk-disarm (ds) =
    let e = dk-flick-end ds
    in "disarmed: watching " & integer-to-text (dk-flick-fid ds)
       & "  none " & integer-to-text desk-focus-none
       & "  ended " & fk-yn (dk-flick-fid ds == desk-focus-none)

 THE BASELINE ROLLS ONLY WHEN THE ROLL PERIOD HAS PASSED, and the row asks
 the question the design turns on: what the measured interval is at the
 moment of a release. It must never be shorter than one roll period, because
 a baseline that has just been re-based measures nothing.

 THE TWO SEQUENCES ARE THE SAME POINTER UP TO THE RELEASE, and that is what
 makes the pair readable: they differ only in whether the hand was still
 moving when the button came up, which is the whole of what the gesture
 decides.

 COLLAPSING THE BASELINE TO ONE SAMPLE BREAKS THE POSITIVE ARM AND LEAVES THE
 CONTROL ALONE, measured rather than reasoned, and it is not the failure the
 first draft of this paragraph predicted. A one-deep baseline was expected to
 call a stopped hand fast; what it actually does is report an interval of
 0 ms, which `dk-flick-fast` refuses outright, so EVERY release reads slow and
 the gesture is dead rather than trigger-happy. `still moving` is the row that
 goes red, `stopped` does not move, and a suite without the moving arm would
 have scored a deleted gesture as a pass.

  fk-roll : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  fk-roll (ds) (x) (t) =
    let s = dk-flick-sample ds x 100 t fk-tps
    in "  at " & integer-to-text t & " ms x " & integer-to-text x
       & " rolled " & integer-to-text s
       & " baseline x " & integer-to-text (dk-pt-x (peek-32 ds dk-flick-p1-cell))
       & " age " & integer-to-text (t - peek-32 ds dk-flick-t1-cell) & " ms"

  fk-release : Integer, Text, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> Text
  fk-release (ds) (label) (t) (x) (want) =
    let bp = peek-32 ds dk-flick-p1-cell
    in let el = t - peek-32 ds dk-flick-t1-cell
    in let vx = x - dk-pt-x bp
    in label & ": release at " & integer-to-text t & " ms x " & integer-to-text x
       & " against baseline x " & integer-to-text (dk-pt-x bp)
       & " over " & integer-to-text el & " ms"
       & "  interval at least one roll " & fk-yn (el >= dk-flick-roll)
       & "  fast " & fk-yn (dk-flick-fast vx 0 el fk-tps)
       & "  wanted " & fk-yn want
       & "  " & fk-yn (dk-flick-fast vx 0 el fk-tps == want)

Section: Opening

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let a1 = poke-32 ds dk-wr-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in act
      print-line-uni ("speed " & integer-to-text dk-flick-speed
                      & " reach " & integer-to-text dk-flick-reach
                      & " window " & integer-to-text dk-flick-ms
                      & " roll " & integer-to-text dk-flick-roll)
      print-line-uni (fk-dir "thrown left " (0 - 300) 40 dk-edge-left)
      print-line-uni (fk-dir "thrown right" 300 (0 - 40) dk-edge-right)
      print-line-uni (fk-dir "thrown up   " 40 (0 - 300) dk-edge-top)
      print-line-uni (fk-dir "thrown down " (0 - 40) 300 dk-edge-bottom)
      print-line-uni (fk-dir "the tie     " 200 200 dk-edge-right)
      print-line-uni (fk-fast "a throw    " 60 0 50 fk-tps True)
      print-line-uni (fk-fast "a placement" 20 0 50 fk-tps False)
      print-line-uni (fk-fast "exactly 700" 35 0 50 fk-tps True)
      print-line-uni (fk-fast "no hpet    " 60 0 50 0 False)
      print-line-uni (fk-fast "no interval" 60 0 0 fk-tps False)
      print-line-uni (fk-through "followed  " dk-edge-left (0 - 60) 0 48 True)
      print-line-uni (fk-through "stopped   " dk-edge-left (0 - 10) 0 48 False)
      print-line-uni (fk-through "reversed  " dk-edge-left 60 0 48 False)
      print-line-uni (fk-through "up followed" dk-edge-top 0 (0 - 60) 48 True)
      print-line-uni (fk-through "up stopped " dk-edge-top 0 (0 - 10) 48 False)
      print-line-uni (fk-through "down       " dk-edge-bottom 0 60 48 True)
      print-line-uni (fk-through "right      " dk-edge-right 60 0 48 True)
      print-line-uni (fk-verdict "still going" dk-edge-left (0 - 10) 0 100 fk-tps dk-flick-wait)
      print-line-uni (fk-verdict "reached    " dk-edge-left (0 - 60) 0 100 fk-tps dk-flick-dock)
      print-line-uni (fk-verdict "gave up    " dk-edge-left (0 - 10) 0 400 fk-tps dk-flick-drop)
      print-line-uni (fk-verdict "late reach " dk-edge-left (0 - 60) 0 400 fk-tps dk-flick-drop)
      print-line-uni (fk-verdict "no hpet    " dk-edge-left (0 - 10) 0 100 0 dk-flick-drop)
      print-line-uni (fk-arm ds desk-focus-clkp 1300 7)
      print-line-uni (fk-disarm ds)
      let b = dk-flick-begin ds 100 100 0
      in print-line-uni "the baseline, pointer moving throughout:"
      print-line-uni (fk-roll ds 200 60)
      print-line-uni (fk-roll ds 300 120)
      print-line-uni (fk-roll ds 400 180)
      print-line-uni (fk-release ds "still moving" 180 400 True)
      let b2 = dk-flick-begin ds 100 100 0
      in print-line-uni "the same pointer, stopping before the release:"
      print-line-uni (fk-roll ds 200 60)
      print-line-uni (fk-roll ds 300 120)
      print-line-uni (fk-roll ds 400 180)
      print-line-uni (fk-roll ds 400 240)
      print-line-uni (fk-roll ds 400 300)
      print-line-uni (fk-release ds "stopped     " 300 400 False)
    end

